<section xml:id="sec-ch04-08-TripleIntegralsCartesian" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Triple Integrals in Cartesian Coordinates</title>

  <note>
    <p>
      We can use double integrals to calculate the net volume bounded by a
      surface(2-dimensional object, embedded in 3-dimensional space)
      <m>f(x, y)</m> and the <m>x y</m>-plane on a region <m>R</m> in the
      <m>x y</m>-plane.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now, given a function <m>f(x, y, z)</m> (3-dimensional object,
      embedded in 4-dimensional space) and a region <m>D</m> in its domain
      (xyz-space), we will derive a way to calculate the 4-dimensional net
      hyper-volume bounded by <m>f(x, y, z)</m> and <m>x y z</m>-space on
      <m>D</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <p>
    In summary, we will partition the 4 -dimensional net volume into
    4 -dimensional net volumes of 4D rectangular prisms. The process is as
    follows:
  </p>

  <p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Partition the region of integration <m>D</m> in
          <m>x y z</m>-space into rectangular prisms.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-08-rectangular-prism">
          <caption>The region <m>D</m> and a typical rectangular prism (from openstax)</caption>

          <image source="/images/2025_10_28_7d65a1e4b779d77153fbg-01.jpg" width="50%">
            <shortdescription>The region D and a typical rectangular prism.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>The region <m>D</m> and a typical rectangular prism.</p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Denote the volume of the <m>k</m>-th rectangular prism as
          <m>\Delta V_{k}=\Delta z_{k} \Delta y_{k} \Delta x_{k}</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\Delta V_{k}</m> is the volume of the <q>base</q> of the
                <m>k</m>-th 4 -dimensional prism.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Pick a point ( <m>x_{k}, y_{k}, z_{k}</m> ) in the <m>k</m>-th
          rectangular prism and evaluate
          <m>f\left(x_{k}, y_{k}, z_{k}\right)</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>f\left(x_{k}, y_{k}, z_{k}\right)</m> is the
                <q>height</q> of the <m>k</m>-th 4 -dimensional prism.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>

  <p>
    Now, we can approximate the 4 -dimensional net volume bounded by
    <m>f(x, y, z)</m> and <m>x y z</m>-space, restricted to <m>D</m>, by
    adding up the 4 -dimensional net volumes of the prisms. That is,
    <md>
      V \approx \sum_{k=1}^{n} f\left(x_{k}, y_{k}, z_{k}\right) \Delta V_{k}
    </md>
    and
    <md>
      V=\lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{n} f\left(x_{k}, y_{k}, z_{k}\right) \Delta V_{k}=\iiint_{D} f(x, y, z) d V
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    We call this net volume the triple integral of <m>f(x, y, z)</m> over
    <m>D</m>.
  </p>

  <note>
    <p>
      We will evaluate triple integrals by reducing the problem to a double
      integral, with an extra step.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Evaluating Triple Integrals</title>

    <p>
      To integrate a continuous function <m>f(x, y, z)</m> over a
      3 -dimensional region <m>D</m>, rewrite the integral as follows:
      <md>
        \iiint_{D} f(x, y, z) d V=\iint_{R}\left(\int_{G(x, y)}^{H(x, y)} f(x, y, z) d z\right) d A
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Here, <m>R</m> is the projection of the 3 -dimensional region
      <m>D</m> into the <m>x y</m> plane.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <remark>
    <p>
      To find the bounds of integration for
      <m>\iiint_{D} f(x, y, z) d V</m> :
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-08-solid-d">
      <caption>The solid D between the surfaces z equals H of x and y, and z equals G of x and y, above the region R.</caption>

      <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_28_7d65a1e4b779d77153fbg-03. -->
      <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-08-solid-d" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
        <instructions>
          <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
          <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
        </instructions>
        <static>
          <image source="/images/2025_10_28_7d65a1e4b779d77153fbg-03.jpg">
            <shortdescription>The solid D between the surfaces z equals H of x and y, and z equals G of x and y, above the region R.</shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>The solid <m>D</m> between the surfaces <m>z=H(x, y)</m>, and <m>z=G(x, y)</m>, above the region <m>R</m>.</p>
            </description>
          </image>
        </static>
      </interactive>
    </figure>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Draw vertical lines parallel to the <m>z</m>-axis (from the
            negative <m>z</m> to the positive <m>z</m> direction).
          </p>

          <p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Wherever these lines enter the region <m>D</m>, this is
                  the lower bound of integration <m>G(x, y)</m> with respect
                  to <m>z</m>.
                </p>
              </li>

              <li>
                <p>
                  Wherever these lines exit the region <m>D</m>, this is
                  the upper bound of integration <m>H(x, y)</m> with respect
                  to <m>z</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Project (cast a shadow) of the 3 -dimensional region <m>D</m>
            into the <m>x y</m>-plane.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Set up bounds for the other two integrals as you would do any
            other double integral.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            In the end, your triple integral should have the form (depending
            on order of integration):
            <md>
              \iiint_{D} f(x, y, z) d V=\int_{a}^{b} \int_{g(x)}^{h(x)} \int_{G(x, y)}^{H(x, y)} f(x, y, z) d z d y d x
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <title>Volume as a Triple Integral</title>

    <p>
      In order to compute the volume of a 3-dimensional solid <m>D</m>, we
      can use the triple integral
      <md>
        \iiint_{D} 1 d V
      </md>
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-08-ex-21-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the mass of a solid box bounded by the planes <m>x=0</m> and
        <m>x=3</m>, <m>y=0</m> and <m>y=1, z=0</m> and <m>z=2</m>. The
        density (mass per unit volume) of the box is given by
        <m>\rho(x, y, z)=x^2ze^{xyz}</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-08-ex-21-1">
        <caption>The solid box bounded by the planes x equals 0 and x equals 3, y equals 0 and y equals 1, z equals 0 and z equals 2.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_28_7d65a1e4b779d77153fbg-04. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-08-ex-21-1" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_28_7d65a1e4b779d77153fbg-04.png">
              <shortdescription>The solid box bounded by the planes x equals 0 and x equals 3, y equals 0 and y equals 1, z equals 0 and z equals 2.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The solid box bounded by the planes <m>x=0</m> and <m>x=3</m>, <m>y=0</m> and <m>y=1</m>, <m>z=0</m> and <m>z=2</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The box in <m>x y z</m>-space
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The Mass can be calculated  by sectioning the volume into small,
        manageable shapes, usually cubes and multiplying the volume of each
        cube with its density. Since the density is a non constant
        function, instead of a discrete sum we use the integral, which
        after all, is a the limit of a sum with infinite terms. Notice that
        since the bounds of integration for all variables are constants,
        one can freely choose the order of integration it suits them. Since
        it appears that integrating with respect to <m>y</m> first is more
        manageable the integral is set up this way.
      </p>

      <p>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\text{Mass} = \iiint_D \rho (x,y,z)dV = \int_0^3\int_0^2\int_0^1 x^2ze^{xyz} dy dz dx =</mrow>
          <mrow>u=xyz,\quad du = xz dy, \quad y= 0 \Rightarrow u = 0, \quad y =1 \Rightarrow u =xz</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^3\int_0^2\int_0^{xz} xe^u du dz dx = \int_0^3\int_0^2\left . xe^u\right |_0^{xz}  dy dz = \int_0^3\int_0^2 xe^{xz}-x dzdx =</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^3\int_0^2 xe^{xz} dzdx -\int_0^3\int_0^2 xdzdx = \int_0^3\int_0^2 xe^{xz} dzdx  -\int_0^3 2x dz = \int_0^3\int_0^2 xe^{xz} dzdx   - 9  =</mrow>
          <mrow>w=xz,\quad dw = x dz, \quad z= 0 \Rightarrow w = 0, \quad z =2 \Rightarrow w =2x</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^3 \int_0^{2x} e^w dw -9 dx = \int_0^3 \left . e^w \right |_0^{2x} dz -9 =\int_0^3 e^{2x}- 1 dx -9 = \left . \frac{1}{2} e^{2x} -x \right |_0^3 - 9 =</mrow>
          <mrow>\frac{1}{2}e^6 - \frac{1}{2}  -3+0 -9 = \frac{1}{2}e^6-\frac{25}{2}</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-08-ex-21-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Use a triple integral to find the volume of the solid in the first
        octant ( <m>x, y, z \geq 0</m> ) bounded by the plane
        <m> x+2 y+ z=4</m> and the coordinate planes.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-08-ex-21-2">
        <caption>The solid in the first octant bounded by the plane x plus 2 y plus 3 z equals 4.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_28_7d65a1e4b779d77153fbg-06. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-08-ex-21-2" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_28_7d65a1e4b779d77153fbg-06.png">
              <shortdescription>The solid in the first octant bounded by the plane x plus 2 y plus 3 z equals 4.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The solid in the first octant bounded by the plane <m>x+2y+3z=4</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The coordinate plane equations are <m>z=0</m>, <m>x=0</m>, and
        <m>y=0</m>. The plane <m> x+2 y+ z=4</m> or <m>z = 4- 2y-x</m> and
        <m>z=0</m> intersect at the line <m>4-x-2y= 0</m> of the
        <m>xy</m>-plane. For the intercepts of this line we have:
        <m>x= 0 \Rightarrow 4 -2y= 0\Rightarrow (0,2)</m> and
        <m>y=0 \Rightarrow 4-x = 0  \Rightarrow (4,0)</m>. From those points
        we see that the line segment we are interested at is in the first
        quadrant (the finite portion of this line). To see whether the
        plane <m>z = 4- 2y-x</m> is above or below the <m>z=0</m>, we need a
        point in the triangle defined by the <m>x</m>-axis, the
        <m>y</m>-axis, and the previous line segment. Chose a value of
        <m>x</m> within the bounds of the triangle, say <m>x=2</m>. Now to
        be within the triangle, this point has to have <m>y</m> coordinate
        less than the corresponding one of the line <m>4-2y-x = 0</m>
        <m>x=2</m> and <m>0 = 4- 2y-x</m> gives the point <m>(2,0.5)</m>.
        For these <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> coordinates the <m>z</m> coordinate
        of the plane  <m>z = 4- 2y-x</m> is
        <m>z=4 -2 \cdot 0.5 - 2 = 4-3 =1 \gt 0</m> and hence our plane is
        above the plane <m>z=0</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hence the bounds of integration with respect to <m>z</m> are
        <m>0 </m> as the lower one and <m>4-2y-x </m> as the upper one.
        Since we now that our line segment is is in the first quadrant we
        can solve <m>4-x-2y =0</m> for <m>y</m> and acquire the lower bound
        <m>0</m> and the upper bound <m>2-\frac{x}{2}</m> for <m>y</m>.
        Lastly for <m>x</m> we have <m>0</m> and <m>4</m> from the
        intercept of the line and the <m>x</m>-axis, calculated above.
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\text{Volume }= \int_0^4 \int_0^{2-\frac{x}{2}}\int_0^{4-x-2y} 1 dz dy dz = \int_0^4 \int_0^{2-\frac{x}{2}} 4 -x -2y dy dx = \int_0^4 \left . 4y -xy -y^2 \right |_0^{2-\frac{x}{2}}</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^4  4(2-\frac{x}{2}) -x(2-\frac{x}{2}) -(2-\frac{x}{2})^2  dx = \int_0^4 4 -2x +\frac{x^2}{4} dx = \left . 4x-x^2+\frac{x^3}{12}\right |_0^4 = \frac{16}{3}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-08-ex-21-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up (but do not evaluate) a triple integral to find the volume
        of the solid between the sphere <m>x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=5</m> and the
        cone <m>z^{2}-4x^{2}-4y^{2}=0</m>, for <m>z \geq 0</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-08-ex-21-3">
        <caption>The solid between the sphere x squared plus y squared plus z squared equals 5 and the cone z squared minus 4 x squared minus 4 y squared equals 0, for z greater or equal to 0.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_28_7d65a1e4b779d77153fbg-08. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-08-ex-21-3" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_28_7d65a1e4b779d77153fbg-08.png">
              <shortdescription>The solid between the sphere x squared plus y squared plus z squared equals 5 and the cone z squared minus 4 x squared minus 4 y squared equals 0, for z greater or equal to 0.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The solid between the sphere <m>x^2+y^2+z^2=5</m> and the cone <m>z^2-4x^2-4y^2=0</m>, for <m>z \geq 0</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Since we have <m>z\geq 0</m> the shape is above the <m>xy</m>-plane.
        The intersection of the two surfaces is given by
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\left. \begin{matrix} x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}\amp = \amp 5 \\ z^{2}-4x^{2}-4y^{2}\amp = \amp 0 \end{matrix} \right \} \Rightarrow \left. \begin{matrix} z^{2}\amp = \amp 5 - y^2 -x^2\\ z^{2}\amp = \amp 4x^{2}+4y^{2} \end{matrix} \right \} \Rightarrow 5 - y^2 -x^2 =  4x^{2}+4y^{2} \Rightarrow</mrow>
          <mrow>5x^2+5y^2 = 5 \Rightarrow x^2+y^2=1</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Hence the region of the <m>xy</m>-plane that the integral will be
        over is the unit disk centred at the origin. Similarly past examples
        we acquire  the bounds <m>-1\leq x \leq 1</m> and
        <m>-\sqrt(1-x^ 2)\leq y \leq \sqrt{1-x^2}</m>. The last thing to be
        determined is if the cone is above the sphere or the other way
        around.
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Using the coordinates <m>x=0</m> and <m>y=0</m> on the sphere
        <m>0^2+0^2+z^2 = 5\overset{z\geq 0}{\Rightarrow} z= \sqrt 5</m>
        while for the cone <m>z^2-0^2-0^2 = 0 \Rightarrow z= 0</m>, hence
        the sphere is above the cone. This part of the sphere can be
        expressed as <m>z=\sqrt{5-X^2-Y^2}</m> and this part of the cone as
        <m>z=2\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>.
        <md>
          \text{Volume } =\int_{-1}^1\int_{-\sqrt{1-x^2}}^{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\int_{2\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}^{\sqrt{5-x^2-y^2}} 1 dz d y dz
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <title>Changing Order of Integration</title>

    <p>
      When integrating over a 3D region <m>D</m>, you may change the order
      of integration of the iterated integrals (including the
      first/innermost integral). The three things to remember are:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            To find the bounds of integration for the inner integral, draw
            lines parallel to the axis of the variable of integration.
          </p>

          <p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  For example, if integrating in the order
                  <m>d x d z d y</m>, you would draw lines parallel to the
                  <m>x</m>-axis.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Once you are done with the inner integral, you project <m>D</m>
            into the plane containing the other two variables.
          </p>

          <p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  For example, if using the order <m>d x d z d y</m>, you
                  would project <m>D</m> into the <m>y z</m>-plane.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The bounds of integration for each integral (working inside to
            outside) are functions of the next variables of integration.
          </p>

          <p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <p>
                  For example, when using the order <m>d x d y d z</m>, you
                  would get
                  <md>
                    \iiint_{D} f(x, y, z) d V=\int_{m}^{n} \int_{g(z)}^{h(z)} \int_{G(y, z)}^{H(y, z)} f(x, y, z) d x d y d z
                  </md>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-08-ex-21-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Set up (but do not evaluate) a triple integral to find the volume
        of the solid bounded by <m>x=2-y^{2}</m> and <m>x= y^{2}+2 z^{2}</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-08-ex-21-4">
        <caption>The solid bounded by x equals 2 minus y squared and x equals y squared plus 2 z squared.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_28_7d65a1e4b779d77153fbg-11. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-08-ex-21-4" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_28_7d65a1e4b779d77153fbg-11.png">
              <shortdescription>The solid bounded by x equals 2 minus y squared and x equals y squared plus 2 z squared.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The solid bounded by <m>x=2-y^2</m> and <m>x=y^2+2z^2</m>.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The region of integration <m>D</m>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Since <m>x</m> was provided as a function of <m>y</m> and <m>z</m>,
        the triple integral will be set up with the innermost integration
        being with respect to <m>x</m>. For the intersect of the above
        surface one has <m>2-y^2= 2y^2+2^2 \Rightarrow y^2+z^2 = 1</m>.
        Hence the region of the <m>yz</m>-plane that the integral will be
        over is the unit disk centred at the origin. Similarly past examples
        we acquire  the bounds <m>-1\leq z \leq 1</m> and
        <m>-\sqrt(1-z^ 2)\leq z \leq \sqrt{1-z^2}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Using the coordinates <m>y=0</m> and <m>z= 0</m> one sees that
        <m>x=2-y^2</m> is above <m>x=y^2+2z^2</m>.
        <md>
          \text{Volume } =\int_{-1}^1\int_{-\sqrt{1-z^2}}^{\sqrt{1-z^2}}\int_{y^2+2z^2}^{2-y^2} 1 dx d y dz
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
